> Basic File Modification in the Terminal

Modifying files and directories is a fundamental skill. SNAPS-OS includes micro and nano for editing, plus standard commands for creating, copying, moving, and deleting.

### Creating Files and Directories

`mkdir <dirname>`: creates a new directory.
`mkdir -p <path/to/dir>`: creates nested directories (creates parents too).

`touch <filename>`: creates an empty file or updates an existing file's timestamp.

### Copying and Moving

`cp <source> <destination>`: copies a file.
`cp -r <source> <destination>`: copies directories recursively.
use wildcard (*) to copy multiple files: `cp -r projects/* ~/backups/`

`mv <source> <destination>`: moves or renames a file/directory.
combine rename and move: `mv oldname.txt ~/documents/newname.txt`

### Deleting

`rm <filename>`: deletes a file (hard delete, not recoverable).
`rm -r <dirname>`: deletes a directory and all contents recursively.
`rmdir <dirname>`: deletes an empty directory only.

be careful with rm - it performs a hard delete.

### Editing Files

`micro <filename>`: modern, user-friendly editor. saves with ctrl+S, quits with ctrl+Q.
micro is the default editor used in SNAPS-OS scripts.

`nano <filename>`: simple classic editor. saves with ctrl+O, quits with ctrl+X.

### Writing to Files

`echo "text" > file.txt`: overwrites file with text (creates if missing).
`echo "text" >> file.txt`: appends text to file.

`command | tee file.txt`: writes output to file AND displays in terminal.
`command | tee -a file.txt`: appends to file.
